A||N||2||KissMySSFiero||Is a 10 x 30 garage big enough for an engine swap?||||1||
Z||000000||KissMySSFiero||12-31-2001||10:46 AM||||well ive found a place to work on my cars that doesnt take up any reletives garage.  10 x 30 storge shed with electric.  $100 a month and you can work on vehicles in the garage.  only problem i see is the 10ft wide.  is that enough to get an engine out thru the wheel well?  or would i need to lift the car higher and slide the cradle out the rear?  the garage is 14ft high so thats not an issue.  any help is much appreciated.  <P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <BR>Two 88FORMULAS Red & Yellow<BR>        <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>         <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||BN Boomer||12-31-2001||10:49 AM||||I could do an engine swap in a 10x30 garage.<P>Check it out:<P> <IMG SRC="http://wsphotofews.excite.com/004/fL/xH/mZ/IO51930.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||webbee||12-31-2001||11:16 AM||||I've removed the cradle in that size garage. As I recall after the cradle is removed there isn't much room left. Once you separate the engine from the tranny you will probably have to get rid of the engine you are removing to have room for the new engine. No problem if you are rebuilding or pulling as BN Boomer's pic shows. I think removing the cradle is easier to do. If you choose to remove the cradle, take the axles off first, so that you don't have to pay for a 4 wheel alignment. You will have to remove them with the rear outside the garage and then push the car inside. It's a bit of a challenge but you can do it. Good luck!||||reg||1
